A||||3||Bruce||Speakers
Z||000000||Bruce||07-20-99||11:19 AM||||What speakers produce the best sound and fit easily into the existing mounting areas? How much trouble is it to remove the rear speakers?<br>Thanks.
Z||000001||batboy||07-20-99||02:06 PM||||Bruce, I'm not sure there is any one correct answer on your "best speaker" question. Even true stereo experts (I'm not one) argue about that. How much do you want to spend? I'll assume that you're not made of money and don't require the state-of-the-art, top-of-the-line-stuff. On the other hand, don't get the no-name, el cheapo speakers either.<p>How much power (watts) are you planning on running? Some speakers sound great running at normal power, like what your standard in-dash stereo puts out, but others sound best if you have a powerful amp pushing them. Since the Fiero has such a small cockpit, for normal usage, you really don't need a power amp unless you are planning on running a subwoofer.<p>Ok, for the speakers to fit into the factory locations, you need a pair of 4X10 inchers up front in the dash. The front dash grills just pop out if you carefully use a small screwdriver to pry them up. The speaker is held in by 4 screws.<p>The early Fieros (84 and 85, I think) had the rear speakers inside the headrests of the seats. Those are super hard to upgrade and not really worth the effort. Later Fieros had a pair of 4X6 inchers in the upper interior sail trim panel right behind the shoulder belt attachment.<p>These are a little more work to replace than the front ones, but not hard if you take your time and don't force anything. Don't try to remove the grill like I did, or you'll break them (oops). Remove the upper shoulder belt attachment torque head bolt, remove the screw on the edge closest to the door, carefully pop out the hidden plastic fastener near the top front of the trim panel (like many door panels have), and then remove the whole panel (don't forget to reach in when it's loose and unplug the speaker wires).<p>For nice 2 or 3 way premium speakers, expect to pay from $60 to $120 for a pair. I recommend buying all the same kind (brand) for front and rear for the best matching sound. Shop around, but beware of fast talking, slick salespeople working on commission.<p>I've had good luck with the Kicker Impulse speakers (and they sound great too) that I've had in my 87 for 3 years, but I haven't been able to find them around lately. There are people who will argue with me, but the following name brand speakers all have a good reputation:  JBL, Kenwood, Pioneer, Polk, and Infinity.<p>The last pair of Jensen's I had were crappy and didn't last long. I just installed a pair of Pioneer TS-A4103 4X10's (from Best Buy for $80) in the front of my 85 and they sound real good, a whole bunch better than those cheapie factory originals. Don't know yet how well they'll hold up in the long run though.<p>Oh yeah, you'll probably have to either slice in different speaker wire terminals on your factory wires or buy an adapter plug at a stereo shop. When you order speakers from Crutchfield's, they include this adapter. The adapter is nice if you ever want to sell your car and want to put the original speakers back in and keep your newer ones. Hope this helps you decide what to do.
Z||000002||lowCG||07-20-99||07:18 PM||||Yeah,you can spend a WHOLE lot,but get 90% of the same effect with about 20% of the $ spent on the mega-expensive gear,just depends on how demanding your ears are to quality,and where you strike a balance between cost/performance.<br>Poineer is a good deal these days,as are the names mentioned in the previous post.I've had sales people try and tell me all kinds of things,never listen,to them anyways,just the gear and my personal judgement.<br>Quality is going to be more important in such a small cabin than total power.<br>(very much a personal thing,these stereos)<br>If you're handy,there's a place for a decent 6",or 8" woofer under the passenger side dash,my new car will require that I move a couple of relays in order to put my old box up under my dash,however.A smaller size box should work better sealed,without ports,in most cases.<br>My dream system involves getting another 400watt Sony amp,and running it with the one I have to power a Soundstream Reference Series 8"(dual voicecoil),in the box I have,and using the 60watt amp I have to power my MB Quart system(4"midrange,titanium tweeters,crossovers),but I'm not that picky,would rather listen to a rad motor anyways most of the time.
Z||000003||DJRice||07-21-99||10:02 AM||||I'll vouch for the Pioneer Speakers, not that they need it. I put some 4x10s in my Fiero a year ago and they still sound great.  I also replaced the speakers in my Buick (4x6, 6x9). After 5 years they still sound great. I do recommend bass blockers for the 4x6s because they just werent made to "hit"<p>Try crutchfield.  They not only offer a decent price, it may not be the lowest, but you will get pictures describing the installation of whatever you purchase, and quick disconnects so you dont have to cut your wiring harnesses.  I have done lots of business with crutchfield and they have always treated me right and provided all the stuff do do the job quickly and easily.
